It's been my pet peeve for a lot time that RPGs don't really support
thief classes. I mean - they _exist_, but they really just end up
being another kind of fighter. The thief who sneaks around,
deliberately avoiding confrontation, stealing what he needs will
eventually face a major problem: XP.

You have to kill things to get XP.

Okay, so pacifists can get by without it, but for most of us mortals
this makes the 'sneaky' approach more or less impossible.

The problem is that it is programmatically very difficult to measure
"sneakiness". XP for killing monsters is easy: when the monster is
dies, you award XP. Even here there is a weakness - should you get XP
for monsters that are killed by pets or traps or conflict? But for the
most part, this is an acceptable and easily measured solution.

How do we measure success as a thief?

One thing I've been working on is partial XP-awards for incapacitating
a monster temporarily. So if you put a monster to sleep for a
significant amount of time, you get, say, half its XP. Later, if you
kill it, you get the other half. I've coded this up and it isn't too
difficult. Still, you have to deliberately confront monsters, and you
still don't get all the XP unless you get the kill.

Another approach would be to put XP awards on treasure, like original
D&D did (and may still do, I haven't read the manuals for years). What
if we awarded XP for every item, based perhaps on a fraction of its
base monetary value. Of course, we'd need to tag items for which XP
had already been awarded, so you couldn't just keep dropping and
picking up stuff.

This approach would reward successful thievery. If we added a "steal"
skill, along the same lines as the nymph's ability (as others have
done before), then thieves could possibly make a decent amount of XP
by stealing what they need. Of course, it would take a lot of tuning
to make this fit with the existing XP system.

Still, the thief would be disadvantaged with regard to other players
who killed everything AND took the treasure. Hmm. What we need is a
different way to get monster XP without necessarily having to fight
it. Something of equivalent difficulty, but requiring a different kind
of specialisation.

I'm just thinking out loud here. I'm slowly putting together (some of)
these ideas into a patch. If anyone has any good suggestions, I'd like
to hear them.

